WEBVTT

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:13.192
In 1872, the French writer Jules Verne published a famous novel: Around the World in Eighty

00:00:13.192 --> 00:00:14.645
Days.

00:00:14.655 --> 00:00:19.884
The hero is Phileas Fogg, a gentleman in London who lives by the clock.

00:00:19.892 --> 00:00:25.016
Fogg bets half his money that he can travel around the whole world in eighty days.

00:00:25.024 --> 00:00:31.550
Tonight — Wednesday, the second of October, 1872 — at a quarter to nine, his train leaves

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:32.976
London.

00:00:32.984 --> 00:00:36.305
We follow his route, starting today.

00:00:36.313 --> 00:00:39.437
Look at the map: only London is lit.

00:00:39.445 --> 00:00:42.582
Day by day, we light the rest.

00:00:42.589 --> 00:00:47.555
One more name: Passepartout, Fogg's new French valet.

00:00:47.563 --> 00:00:50.082
His watch is four minutes slow.

00:00:50.089 --> 00:00:52.121
Remember the watch.

00:00:52.129 --> 00:00:55.924
First stop: Fogg's street, Savile Row.

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:02.322
This is Savile Row, a quiet street in Mayfair.

00:01:02.332 --> 00:01:04.522
The novel starts here.

00:01:04.529 --> 00:01:09.390
Phileas Fogg lives on this street — at No. 7, the book says.

00:01:09.398 --> 00:01:13.153
Tonight he will leave from that door to go round the world.

00:01:13.161 --> 00:01:19.048
Look in the shop windows: rolls of cloth, chalk marks, half-made jackets.

00:01:19.056 --> 00:01:23.298
In 1872 this was already a street of tailors.

00:01:23.306 --> 00:01:28.622
The firm says that in 1865 they made a short evening jacket for a prince — the first dinner

00:01:28.622 --> 00:01:30.811
jacket, they say.

00:01:30.819 --> 00:01:37.214
One more stop: on the roof of No. 3, in January 1969, the Beatles played their famous

00:01:37.214 --> 00:01:39.219
rooftop concert.

00:01:39.227 --> 00:01:42.587
And one small secret about No. 7.

00:01:42.595 --> 00:01:47.272
Verne wrote that the playwright Sheridan died there in 1814.

00:01:47.279 --> 00:01:52.561
In fact Sheridan lived at No. 14, and died in 1816.

00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:58.277
Fogg's day, hour by hour.

00:01:58.288 --> 00:02:00.346
He gets up at eight.

00:02:00.354 --> 00:02:04.004
Tea and toast at twenty-three minutes past eight.

00:02:04.012 --> 00:02:10.517
Shaving water at nine thirty-seven, at exactly thirty degrees — eighty-six Fahrenheit.

00:02:10.525 --> 00:02:14.385
He leaves at half past eleven; home at midnight.

00:02:14.393 --> 00:02:20.819
This morning, at twenty-nine minutes past eleven, Passepartout walked into this house.

00:02:20.827 --> 00:02:24.188
Then ten English houses in five years.

00:02:24.196 --> 00:02:26.806
He wants a quiet life now.

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:34.017
From Savile Row, Fogg walks to his club and counts his steps.

00:02:34.028 --> 00:02:37.586
One thousand one hundred and fifty-one steps.

00:02:37.594 --> 00:02:39.717
He never hurries.

00:02:39.725 --> 00:02:43.362
We are now on Pall Mall, walking west.

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:47.046
This is clubland — a street of private clubs.

00:02:47.054 --> 00:02:52.283
It was one of the first streets in London with gas lamps, in 1807.

00:02:52.291 --> 00:02:58.928
Watch the left side: the Athenaeum at No. 107, then the Travellers at No. 106.

00:02:58.936 --> 00:03:05.212
Then the grand building with tall windows: the Reform Club, No. 104, by Charles Barry,

00:03:05.212 --> 00:03:07.704
opened 1841.

00:03:07.712 --> 00:03:12.099
Fogg counted 1,151 steps.

00:03:12.107 --> 00:03:14.757
We measure about one kilometre.

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:23.662
It is real, and still here — 104 Pall Mall, since 1836.

00:03:23.672 --> 00:03:30.585
In the book, Fogg spends every day here until midnight: meals, newspapers, cards.

00:03:30.593 --> 00:03:35.322
The architect Charles Barry finished the building in 1841.

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:41.533
Stone outside; inside, a grand hall two floors high, under a glass roof.

00:03:41.541 --> 00:03:44.098
Look up at the tall windows.

00:03:44.106 --> 00:03:50.046
Here Fogg ate his breakfast: grilled fish with Reading sauce — remember that sauce.

00:03:50.054 --> 00:03:55.388
Roast beef, a fruit tart, cheese, and the club's famous tea.

00:03:55.396 --> 00:04:00.725
Now three pictures: the front today, the architect's drawing, and the great hall, drawn

00:04:00.725 --> 00:04:03.033
in 1841.

00:04:03.041 --> 00:04:06.125
No camera has been inside for us.

00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:12.745
Verne describes the inside of the club with much detail.

00:04:12.756 --> 00:04:14.932
Mosaic floors.

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:19.682
A gallery under a dome, held up by twenty red stone columns.

00:04:19.690 --> 00:04:22.090
Blue painted windows.

00:04:22.098 --> 00:04:25.800
A dining room with nine windows onto a garden.

00:04:25.808 --> 00:04:28.577
Which of these details is real?

00:04:28.585 --> 00:04:32.235
A dining-room with nine windows onto the garden.

00:04:32.243 --> 00:04:33.879
Exact.

00:04:33.887 --> 00:04:39.564
Nine tall casement windows in the coffee-room's south wall, and the garden is still there.

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:44.725
Evening, at the club.

00:04:44.736 --> 00:04:49.965
Fogg and five friends play whist, their card game, like every night.

00:04:49.973 --> 00:04:54.723
In the story — only in the story — a thief has stolen fifty-five thousand pounds from

00:04:54.723 --> 00:04:56.662
the Bank of England.

00:04:56.670 --> 00:05:01.881
And a newspaper, the Daily Telegraph, has counted: you can now go around the world in

00:05:01.881 --> 00:05:03.596
eighty days.

00:05:03.604 --> 00:05:06.596
At the table, this becomes a bet.

00:05:06.604 --> 00:05:11.978
Andrew Stuart, an engineer, bets four thousand pounds that it cannot be done.

00:05:11.986 --> 00:05:16.846
Fogg bets twenty thousand pounds — half his money — that it can.

00:05:16.854 --> 00:05:19.794
Look at the card: six names.

00:05:19.802 --> 00:05:22.702
Stuart first, Fogg last.

00:05:22.709 --> 00:05:27.749
Fogg must be back in this same room on Saturday, the twenty-first of December, at a quarter

00:05:27.749 --> 00:05:29.728
to nine at night.

00:05:29.736 --> 00:05:32.162
Exactly eighty days.

00:05:32.170 --> 00:05:34.531
It really was a Saturday.

00:05:34.538 --> 00:05:38.412
Fogg's motto: 'The unforeseen does not exist.'

00:05:38.420 --> 00:05:43.215
Fogg's bank, Baring's, closed in 1995.

00:05:43.223 --> 00:05:45.688
The bet has outlived the bank.

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:51.121
How could anyone believe this plan?

00:05:51.132 --> 00:05:54.203
Because the world had just become smaller.

00:05:54.211 --> 00:05:58.776
When one of the card players said the world was big, Fogg answered with three words: '

00:05:58.776 --> 00:06:00.545
It was once.'

00:06:00.553 --> 00:06:02.874
Look at the three lights.

00:06:02.882 --> 00:06:06.532
The Suez Canal opened in 1869.

00:06:06.539 --> 00:06:12.084
The American railroad, coast to coast, was joined in 1869.

00:06:12.092 --> 00:06:16.755
The last rail link across India closed the gap in 1870.

00:06:16.763 --> 00:06:22.334
Three doors, all opened within ten months, two years before tonight.

00:06:22.342 --> 00:06:24.689
Now watch the line draw.

00:06:24.697 --> 00:06:27.808
London to Suez: seven days.

00:06:27.816 --> 00:06:31.597
Suez to Bombay, by ship: thirteen.

00:06:31.605 --> 00:06:35.387
Bombay to Calcutta, by rail: three.

00:06:35.395 --> 00:06:38.532
Calcutta to Hong Kong: thirteen.

00:06:38.539 --> 00:06:41.650
Hong Kong to Yokohama: six.

00:06:41.658 --> 00:06:45.637
Yokohama to San Francisco: twenty-two.

00:06:45.645 --> 00:06:50.005
San Francisco to New York, by rail: seven.

00:06:50.013 --> 00:06:53.032
New York back to London: nine.

00:06:53.039 --> 00:06:55.203
Add them up: eighty.

00:06:57.820 --> 00:07:00.286
Twenty-five past seven.

00:07:00.296 --> 00:07:02.894
Fogg comes home from the club.

00:07:02.902 --> 00:07:08.644
He calls Passepartout and says: 'We start for Dover and Calais in ten minutes.'

00:07:08.652 --> 00:07:13.683
Dover is on the English coast; Calais is across the sea, in France.

00:07:13.691 --> 00:07:19.749
Where to? 'We are going round the world.' 'In eighty days.'

00:07:19.757 --> 00:07:21.880
Now help him pack.

00:07:21.888 --> 00:07:25.249
Drag in what goes; leave what stays.

00:07:25.257 --> 00:07:28.815
Two shirts and three pairs of stockings each.

00:07:28.823 --> 00:07:31.736
A mackintosh and a travelling-cloak.

00:07:31.744 --> 00:07:37.052
The red-bound Bradshaw — a thick book of train times, hundreds of pages.

00:07:37.059 --> 00:07:41.920
And a fat roll of Bank of England notes: twenty thousand pounds.

00:07:41.928 --> 00:07:44.894
No books — the house has none.

00:07:44.902 --> 00:07:47.881
The electric clock stays on the mantel.

00:07:47.888 --> 00:07:52.170
And so, tonight, does the gas — remember that.

00:07:52.178 --> 00:07:56.920
Two shirts, three pairs of stockings, and half a fortune.

00:07:59.540 --> 00:08:04.632
Fogg and Passepartout take a cab at the end of Savile Row — probably a hansom, a fast

00:08:04.632 --> 00:08:06.782
two-wheeled horse cab.

00:08:06.793 --> 00:08:11.679
To the driver, Fogg is a 'swell': a rich, well-dressed man.

00:08:11.687 --> 00:08:14.390
The fare is sixpence a mile.

00:08:14.398 --> 00:08:19.469
Watch the line on the 1872 map, south and east to the Strand.

00:08:19.477 --> 00:08:25.443
London already had an Underground railway — nine years old, full of steam and smoke.

00:08:25.451 --> 00:08:27.864
Fogg takes a cab instead.

00:08:27.872 --> 00:08:34.258
Listen: hooves on stone, and fast French dance music for Passepartout, the Parisian.

00:08:34.266 --> 00:08:37.785
They left the club at twenty-five past seven.

00:08:37.793 --> 00:08:41.245
They reach Charing Cross at twenty past eight.

00:08:43.860 --> 00:08:47.984
This is the Strand, and ahead is Charing Cross station.

00:08:47.994 --> 00:08:52.328
Forget 'old London': for Fogg, this was new London.

00:08:52.336 --> 00:08:57.552
The station opened in January 1864 — eight years old that night.

00:08:57.560 --> 00:09:01.986
The hotel behind the stone cross: 1865.

00:09:01.994 --> 00:09:05.513
The cross itself: 1865.

00:09:05.521 --> 00:09:08.999
The Underground railway: nine years old.

00:09:09.007 --> 00:09:12.105
The Embankment by the river: two.

00:09:12.113 --> 00:09:15.592
Big Ben, the clock tower: thirteen.

00:09:15.599 --> 00:09:19.618
Much of what Fogg passes tonight is younger than his valet.

00:09:19.626 --> 00:09:26.776
One thing you cannot see: the station's huge iron roof, 155 metres in one span.

00:09:26.784 --> 00:09:30.328
It fell in 1905 and was replaced.

00:09:30.336 --> 00:09:32.605
That is all we will say.

00:09:32.613 --> 00:09:35.565
Then the one warm moment of the evening.

00:09:35.573 --> 00:09:38.697
A barefoot woman with a child asks for money.

00:09:38.705 --> 00:09:42.855
Fogg gives her the twenty guineas he has just won at cards.

00:09:42.863 --> 00:09:45.552
He does not even stop walking.

00:09:48.180 --> 00:09:54.422
On the left: a drawing from 1872 — the hotel and forecourt as Fogg saw them.

00:09:54.433 --> 00:09:57.319
On the right: the cross today.

00:09:57.327 --> 00:10:02.201
Six platforms behind it, and still about one train an hour to Dover.

00:10:02.209 --> 00:10:06.069
The hotel is still a hotel, under a new name.

00:10:08.700 --> 00:10:11.903
Twenty to nine, still under the roof.

00:10:11.913 --> 00:10:15.182
Look up — what is the weather doing tonight?

00:10:15.189 --> 00:10:18.603
A dark night and a fine, steady rain.

00:10:18.611 --> 00:10:20.866
Verne's own words.

00:10:20.874 --> 00:10:25.024
Rain on the station roof, then rain on the carriage window.

00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:30.797
It's twenty to nine, and it's raining.

00:10:30.807 --> 00:10:33.036
Who is your new master?

00:10:33.759 --> 00:10:38.811
<v Passepartout>Mr Phileas Fogg of Savile Row, monsieur — the most exact and settled gentleman in the

00:10:38.811 --> 00:10:41.632
<v Passepartout>United Kingdom, they told me.

00:10:41.643 --> 00:10:48.543
<v Passepartout>Rises at eight, tea and toast at twenty-three past, out at half past eleven, home at midnight.

00:10:48.551 --> 00:10:50.424
<v Passepartout>A real machine.

00:10:50.432 --> 00:10:53.267
<v Passepartout>I do not mind serving a machine.

00:10:53.274 --> 00:10:56.990
<v Passepartout>I have known him since twenty-nine minutes past eleven.

00:10:57.732 --> 00:11:00.079
We've got a train to catch.

00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:07.982
This postcard is from 1905, thirty-three years after Fogg.

00:11:07.992 --> 00:11:12.734
The same platforms, and the old roof, months before it fell.

00:11:12.742 --> 00:11:15.563
The boat train to Paris is waiting.

00:11:15.571 --> 00:11:17.853
Now, tonight.

00:11:17.861 --> 00:11:20.879
Twenty to nine: we are seated.

00:11:20.887 --> 00:11:26.221
A quarter to nine: the whistle, and we are off, over Hungerford Bridge.

00:11:26.229 --> 00:11:29.616
Trains still leave Fogg's station this way.

00:11:29.624 --> 00:11:33.537
Our film is daylight; Fogg had rain and dark.

00:11:33.545 --> 00:11:39.076
Look right: the Embankment along the river, two years old in 1872.

00:11:39.084 --> 00:11:42.616
Beyond it, the clock tower, thirteen.

00:11:42.624 --> 00:11:45.721
New London slips away behind him.

00:11:45.729 --> 00:11:49.366
Today this train takes about two hours to Dover.

00:11:49.374 --> 00:11:53.116
Fogg's plan gave him seven days to reach Suez.

00:11:53.124 --> 00:11:55.300
This is the first hour.

00:11:55.308 --> 00:11:59.471
A few miles out, Passepartout sits up straight.

00:11:59.479 --> 00:12:05.189
He has forgotten to turn off the gas in his room. 'It will burn,' says Fogg, 'at your

00:12:05.189 --> 00:12:06.813
expense.'

00:12:06.821 --> 00:12:08.787
To be continued.

00:12:11.420 --> 00:12:16.328
Your souvenir today: Fogg's last breakfast in London — the meal he ate before betting

00:12:16.328 --> 00:12:18.399
half his fortune.

00:12:18.409 --> 00:12:22.520
The dish is grilled fish with a Reading-sauce-style ketchup.

00:12:22.528 --> 00:12:28.915
Reading sauce was a real English sauce from 1802; it disappeared in the 1960s.

00:12:28.923 --> 00:12:30.836
Save the card.

00:12:30.844 --> 00:12:35.120
Cook the meal on the evening of the twenty-first of December, and sit down to eat by a

00:12:35.120 --> 00:12:37.073
quarter to nine.

00:12:37.081 --> 00:12:39.875
That is when Fogg promised to be back.

00:12:39.883 --> 00:12:44.902
His motto, and ours: 'A well-used minimum suffices for everything.'
